        "title": "Ecology, Niches, and Trophic Roles",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Ecological niche theory is the framework for understanding how species partition resources and coexist (or fail to). Hutchinson's hypervolume niche formalization, Lotka-Volterra competition models, and food web theory together describe how energy flows through ecosystems and why particular species combinations are stable. Zoology contributes the animal components — primary and secondary consumers, apex predators, scavengers — to these frameworks."
